  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nAfter a cool start to the day, temperatures have risen into the\nlow 60s at most locations as of early this afternoon. A scattered\nfair weather cu field has popped within the last couple of hours,\nthough conditions remain mostly sunny. Patchy fog may develop \nlate tonight into early Thursday morning as winds decouple and \nskies clear out, though it remains uncertain whether or not it \nwill manifest as fog or low stratus.\n\nNo major changes to speak of from previous forecasts as Hudson Bay\nhigh dominates the eastern CONUS. Persistence forecast more or \nless continues as a result, with ensembles continuing to back off\non rain chances during the weekend. Long-range models attempt to\ngenerate light rain over portions of central to north-central\nWisconsin Saturday afternoon as a weak shortwave traverses the \nupper Mississippi Valley, though what little forcing and moisture\nthere is immediately dissolves upon hitting a barrier of dry air.\nNBM probabilistic guidance shows only a 30 to 40% signal for \nreceiving 0.1\" of QPF over our far western tier of counties \nthrough Sunday morning. Another brief dry spell then settles in\nthrough the early to middle part of next week.\n\nConsensus holds that our next organized system moves through \nsometime during the middle of next week as potent upper trough and\nattendant surface low track through the upper Midwest. With PWATs\nreaching 90th percentile relative to climo and more concrete\nforcing present, initial thoughts point toward this being a heavy\nstratiform rain setup. Will continue to monitor trends throughout\nthe week. Otherwise, temperatures will read mainly in the 60s\nduring the extended, occasionally warming into the low 70s.",
